{"content":[{"c":["ABEL (P",{"c":["lace"],"sourceClass":"lc","t":"span"},")"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Fortified border city in upper Galilee to which King David’s general Joab pursued the rebel Sheba. After a wise woman of the city negotiated with Joab, the citizens executed Sheba and threw his head over the wall. Joab then called off the siege (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.20.13-22","r":"2Sam.20.13-22","rawR":"2Sam.20.13-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.20.13-22","value":"2Sam.20.13-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 20:13-22"},"). The city was later conquered by the Syrian Ben-hadad during a continuing war between King Asa of Judah and King Baasha of Israel. When Asa persuaded Ben-hadad to break a treaty with Baasha, Ben-hadad took a large amount of territory, including Abel, or Abel-beth-maacah, as it was also called (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.15.16-20","r":"1Kgs.15.16-20","rawR":"1Kgs.15.16-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.15.16-20","value":"1Kgs.15.16-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 15:16-20"},"). Still later, Abel-beth-maacah (sometimes called simply Abel of Beth-maacah, or Abel of Beth-maachah) was conquered by Tiglath-pileser III, and its inhabitants were taken captive to Assyria (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.15.29","r":"2Kgs.15.29","rawR":"2Kgs.15.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.15.29","value":"2Kgs.15.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 15:29"},"). The same city is called Abel-maim (“meadow of water”), emphasizing the productivity of the region (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.16.4","r":"2Chr.16.4","rawR":"2Chr.16.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.16.4","value":"2Chr.16.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 16:4"},"). The town has been identified with modern Tell Abil-el-Qamh."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"AbelPlace","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"2 Sm 20:13-22","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.20.13-22","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.20.13-22","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.20.13-22"},{"anchorText":"1 Kgs 15:16-20","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Kgs.15.16-20","rawQueryValue":"1Kgs.15.16-20","sourceRefExact":"1Kgs.15.16-20"},{"anchorText":"2 Kgs 15:29","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.15.29","rawQueryValue":"2Kgs.15.29","sourceRefExact":"2Kgs.15.29"},{"anchorText":"2 Chr 16:4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Chr.16.4","rawQueryValue":"2Chr.16.4","sourceRefExact":"2Chr.16.4"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b3f4137c568ee0fb7b3834dda2b5510873343120c7da32aa8b7ecdbd6a7d7761","sourceItemName":"AbelPlace","sourceItemOrder":18,"sourceMember":"Articles/A.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"AbelPlace","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Abel (Place)","title":"Abel (Place)","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}